/swgOcZieMaYLGBnX8SQDKOzr+AGJgNEMLyzBTmU35egDEFoiWXQ1l/LEAdZLIMc0iOLf6GeMeQD
buzxsYsFKwAHj5fAC6Qphu9tstV5AAAAAElFTkSuQmCC</value></data><data name="labelTS295.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>- Any changes made here will only apply after Thetis is restarted
- If VAC is enabled input will be taken from the VAC and not cmASIO
- If a red cmASIO icon is shown in the status bar there is an issue
- The ASIO device will not be available to VAC if in use by cmASIO
- 48,000 sa/s sampling rate
- sample buffer size is fixed at 64
- Int32LSB sample format (24 bit samples, left aligned in 32 bit little-endian integers)

- Protocol 1 is supported but performance may be degraded compared to Protocol 2
- Remove any previous cmASIO settings from HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SOFTWARE\OpenHPSDR\Thetis-x64
- This now uses HKEY_CURRENT_USER\SOFTWARE\OpenHPSDR\Thetis-x64</value></data>
  <data name="chkReplaceCurrentMeterInSelectedSkin.ToolTip" xml:space="preserve"><value>If this is checked, the downloaded meter skins
will be added to the current selected skin. Unchecked
and the default MultiMeter skins will be updated and
only used if a selected skin does not provide its own.</value></data>
  <data name="lgLinearGradientTX_waterfall.EncodedText" xml:space="preserve"><value></value></data>
  <data name="chkPurgeBuffers.ToolTip" xml:space="preserve"><value>On a mox transition there are times where you get a 'pulse' of energy in the display.
This is not spurious RF but a side effect of the FFT bins and averages.
This setting will reset the WDSP analyzer pixel/average buffers.
They will take a time to 're-fill' and is determined by sample rate and bin size.</value></data>
